![Single-shot spectra from RLs with different cavity lengths]({{ site.baseurl }}/images/persistence_image.jpg)

Random lasers are often characterized by strong pulse-to-pulse spectral fluctuations arising from the complex interplay between disorder, scattering, and gain competition. In this work, we demonstrate that the degree of spectral persistence can be effectively controlled by tuning the cavity length of a random laser operating under pulsed excitation.

Using DNA-based random laser cavities with adjustable length, we observe a clear transition from highly fluctuating spectra to stable and reproducible emission patterns. The key parameter governing this behaviour is the number of cavity round trips that photons can complete during the pump pulse. When only a few round trips occur, successive spectra are largely uncorrelated. In contrast, when photons perform approximately 8–10 round trips while gain is present, mode competition leads to persistent spectral signatures with strong shot-to-shot correlation.

The experimental observations are supported by coupled-mode-theory simulations, which reproduce the transition from unstable to persistent lasing regimes. The results provide a simple physical picture linking pump duration, cavity length, and spectral stability in random lasers.

Beyond their fundamental interest, these findings offer a practical route to engineer the degree of complexity and stability of random laser emission, which is relevant for applications in sensing, imaging, and photonic information processing.
